---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import FeatureRow from "../../components/FeatureRow.astro";
import SubNav from "../../components/SubNav.astro";
import { PLAN_PAGES } from "../../sections";
// The app's own review of COROS's catalogue, counted at build time.
import { EXERCISE_REVIEW, REVIEW_MUSCLE_TABLES } from "../../../../src/calendar/exerciseReview";

const reviewed = Object.keys(EXERCISE_REVIEW).length;
const musclesFixed = Object.keys(REVIEW_MUSCLE_TABLES.corrections).length;
const musclesAdded = Object.keys(REVIEW_MUSCLE_TABLES.uncoded).length;
const equipmentFixed = Object.values(EXERCISE_REVIEW).filter((review) => review.equipment).length;

const corrections = [
  { movement: "Squat, leg press, box step-up", coros: "Hamstrings first", app: "Quads and glutes, hamstrings assisting" },
  { movement: "Battle ropes", coros: "Biceps", app: "Shoulders, arms and core assisting" },
  { movement: "Side plank, twists, woodchops", coros: "Abs only: COROS has no obliques", app: "Obliques first, abs assisting" },
  { movement: "Neck harness work", coros: "Traps: COROS has no neck", app: "Neck, traps assisting" },
  { movement: "TRX movements", coros: "Cable machine", app: "Suspension trainer" },
  { movement: "Battle ropes, sled push and pull", coros: "Others", app: "Rope, sled" },
];
---

<Base og="plan"
  title="Exercises Library"
  description="The Exercises Library in Heracles Records: COROS's whole strength catalogue by body part, muscle or equipment, with every movement's muscles and equipment described again where COROS gets them wrong."
>
  <SubNav pages={PLAN_PAGES} label="Plan and Library" />

  <section class="wrap page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Exercises Library</p>
    <h1>Every movement, and what it really works.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      COROS's whole movement catalogue to read, a third tab in the Training Library. Pick a body
      part on the figure, then a muscle inside it, or an equipment, and each movement shows its
      clip, the muscles it works and how hard.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="feature-text">
      <p class="eyebrow">Browse</p>
      <h2>Find a movement by what it trains.</h2>
      <ul class="ticks columns">
        <li><strong>The body as the filter.</strong> Pick a part on the figure and it zooms in so you can pick a single muscle, or the whole part.</li>
        <li><strong>By equipment</strong>: barbell, dumbbell, kettlebell, cable, bands, suspension, bodyweight and the rest.</li>
        <li><strong>Each movement read in full</strong>: its demonstration clip, the muscles it leads with and those that assist, and how hard it is.</li>
        <li><strong>HYROX</strong>: the eight stations are one toggle away, in race order.</li>
        <li><strong>Favourites</strong>: a heart keeps the movements you use, and narrows the builder's search to them.</li>
        <li><strong>The abductors</strong> are a muscle of their own, no longer counted with the glutes.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Described again</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Where COROS gets a movement wrong, the app says so.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        COROS's catalogue names each movement's muscles and equipment as codes, and some of them
        are wrong. Its muscle list has no obliques and no neck at all. Read as it comes, a side
        plank works no obliques and a squat is mostly hamstrings, and every total by muscle, on the
        Strength screen and in Coach, is skewed with it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="stats">
      <li><strong>{reviewed}</strong><span>movements described again</span></li>
      <li><strong>{musclesFixed}</strong><span>with their muscles corrected</span></li>
      <li><strong>{musclesAdded}</strong><span>given the obliques or the neck</span></li>
      <li><strong>{equipmentFixed}</strong><span>with their equipment fixed</span></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="prose-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Movement</th><th>COROS says</th><th>Heracles Records says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {corrections.map((row) => <tr><td>{row.movement}</td><td>{row.coros}</td><td>{row.app}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Each movement is decided by its COROS key, never by its name: "Bicycle Crunches" says
      nothing of the obliques, "Romanian Deadlift" nothing of the hamstrings. A movement the app
      has not reviewed is exactly as COROS sends it. Think one is wrong? Say so from the app,
      under <strong>Settings → About → Report an issue</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap">
    <FeatureRow shot="22-library-workouts" alt="A strength workout: each movement with COROS's picture and its demonstration clip">
      <p class="eyebrow">Where it is used</p>
      <h2>The same description, everywhere.</h2>
      <p>
        What the Exercises Library says of a movement is what the rest of the app reads: the
        Strength screen's body map and muscle totals, and the muscles Coach is told a session
        worked.
      </p>
      <a class="more" href="/activities/strength/">Strength in Heracles Records</a>
    </FeatureRow>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 0 0 32px;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .stats {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  .stats li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    padding-top: 14px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .stats strong {
    color: var(--accent);
    font: 600 34px/1.1 var(--font-title);
  }

  .stats span {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .prose-table {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .prose-table table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 560px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .prose-table th,
  .prose-table td {
    padding: 12px 16px 12px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .prose-table th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .prose-table td {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .prose-table td:first-child {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .note {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: 15px;
  }
</style>
